WEBVTT

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.210
Bismillah wa alhamdulillah wa salatu wa
salam ala rasulullah. Hello calculus

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.790
students. Today we're going to be taking
derivatives of logarithmic functions. Let's

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.410
get right into it. Okay, let's get into our
logarithmic examples. Just so we know

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.610
the form here, when y equals log a b, y

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:22.030
prime equals 1 over b ln a times b
prime, the exact same as when

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.110
we had ln, when we were taking the derivatives
of ln, but now the only difference

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:31.490
is we're multiplying by this ln of a
factor, just like how when we had

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.850
e to the x, the derivative was e to the x, But
when we had, let's say, a to the x,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.870
it was a to the x times ln a. It's the same
concept. So for our first example here,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.090
if we have y prime, it would take
the form of 1 over 5x

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.470
ln of 4 multiplied by the
derivative of 5x, which is 5.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.090
These 5s will cancel out, and you will
be left with 1 over x ln 4.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:03.270
Next example, let's do our Leibniz
notation. dy over dx here

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.810
would equal d of log 3

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:12.090
5x squared minus 2x all over

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.430
d of 5x squared minus 2x and
this is multiplied by

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.990
d of 5x squared minus 2x over d of x

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.270
This will give us 1 over 5x squared minus 2x

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.250
multiplied by our addition now. When
we're doing log, we need to multiply

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:38.730
by the factor which is ln of 3
because our base is 3

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:44.050
then I'm going to multiply by the

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.110
power rule for 5x squared minus 2x
that would leave us with 10x

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.930
minus 2. If you want to put it on top,
then we would have here

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.010
10x minus 2

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:03.380
over 5x squared minus 2x multiplied by

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:08.780
ln of 3 as our final answer. Going on to
our next example, we have y

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:14.240
equals x cubed times log 5 x. And of course,
we'll be using our power rule for this

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:19.620
example because it follows the form of two
functions in our y function. So

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.340
here, if we take y prime,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.370
we will do the derivative of the first,
which will be 3x squared multiplied

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.810
by log 5 of x, base 5 of x.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.830
And then this we are adding here, x cubed will
stay the same, and we're taking the

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.270
derivative of log 5 x. Log 5 x, the
derivative will be 1

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.690
over x ln 5 multiplied by the

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.990
x to the power of 1 which is just x so we can
just close this bracket here we have

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.490
an x cubed and x squared at the bottom we can
bring this to 2 and we can take this

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:03.470
out this will then simplify to 3x

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.450
squared log

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:11.880
5 x plus x squared over ln 5.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.950
One last thing we can do here is we can do change
of base on our log. Change of base

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.350
on our log tells us that if you have, let's
say, log a b, that is the same

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:23.910
as

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.980
log x of b over log x

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.780
of a. And whenever you have a ln,
all ln is, is just

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.380
log base e to any number.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:46.550
So, using this property, what we can
do is we can take this log

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.030
and make it log 5 x. Instead, it
can be ln x over ln 5.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:04:00.390
Subbing that into here, we will get
3x squared ln x over

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.850
ln 5 plus x squared over ln 5. We can add

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.310
these two now because we have the same
denominator. This will give us 3x squared ln

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.190
of x plus

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:20.930
x squared all over ln of 5 as
our final answer.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:26.230
You can also factor the x squared out, but that is
not required. For our last example here, we have y

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:31.710
equals log base 7 e to the power
of x to the power

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.470
of 5. There's a lot going on here, and I'm
going to make it even more going on

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.170
because I want to take the Leibniz notation
for this question. So here we'll have

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:44.410
dy over dx. It's going to equal d of log

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.770
base 7 e to the power of x to the
power of 5, all of this

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:55.110
will be d e x to the

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.930
power of 5 we're going to multiply this by the
first part of the chain rule which is

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.910
going to be d e the power of x to
the power of 5 all over d

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.530
x to the power of 5 and then we're going to
multiply this by the last component our

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.010
last function because remember this was three
functions we had a function and inside

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.470
this function we had e x to the power 5 inside e
x to the power 5 there was one more

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.990
function this function it will be dx to
the power of 5 over d of x.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.050
Now applying the derivative to each part of our
chain rule in our first one we will

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:31.190
have 1 over e x to the power of 5 times

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.010
ln of 7. Remember again this ln of 7 is a new
addition we have when we're taking the

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.810
derivative of log and that's because we have a
base 7 here. We're going to multiply

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.750
this by the derivative of e x to the power of 5,
and we know that it's always just

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:48.290
going to be e x to the power of 5. Then we're
going to multiply that by the power

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:52.530
rule applied to x to the power of 5, which
would be 5 x to the power of 4.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:59.510
We can go one more. Here, these two will cancel
out, and we'll be left with 5 x to

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.970
the power of 4 all over ln of
7 as our final

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:05.410
answer.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:14.290
Once again, it's pretty easy because we did
already the ln. So we already know

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:19.090
these rules. Taking this part of the chain rule
that we added in isn't really an

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.310
addition because we already did this
once in a previous video.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:28.100
This is actually all of the algebra
for unit three. So I told

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:32.020
you it is actually one of the easier units. And
once you understand the rules of the

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:35.620
algebra, it's pretty, pretty simple. But I will
see you guys in the next episode.
